1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
A long job run as one giant session accumulates noise until the model loses focus.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Splitting it into steps gives you a cleaner option, where each step runs against its own

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:14,000
focused context and passes forward only what the next step needs.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:19,000
This lesson covers how to split it and when to chain explicitly.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:25,000
Let's anchor three terms first. A workflow is a developer-authored sequence with fixed control

6
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,000
while an agent lets the model choose the next action at runtime.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,000
Prompt chaining is how you build a workflow out of model calls.

8
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:39,000
You break a task into sequential steps, where each step's output feeds the next,

9
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:42,000
so you can inspect and control the pipeline.

10
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:47,000
And a staged handoff means passing forward only the distilled output of each stage,

11
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:53,000
not its full working context, so the next step starts focused. Keep these straight.

12
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:56,000
Chaining is the mechanism and the staged handoff

13
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:59,000
is the discipline that keeps each step's context tight.

14
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,000
So why split it all? You can run a long job two ways.

15
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:09,000
One is a single growing session that carries every prior turn forward,

16
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:13,000
noise and all, until the important instructions get diluted.

17
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:18,000
The other is a chain of focused steps, where each stage starts against a tight,

18
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:21,000
relevant context instead of the whole history.

19
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:25,000
In the staged version, each step passes on its distilled output,

20
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:30,000
not its scratch work, so the next step isn't paying for tokens it will never use.

21
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:34,000
Splitting the work is what keeps every step sharp.

22
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:39,000
The long session feels simpler at first, but it degrades as the noise piles up.

23
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:44,000
When a step comes up, you're choosing between isolating it and keeping it in line.

24
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:48,000
And that's the same call we made last lesson, now inside a chain.

25
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:53,000
Heavy throwaway noise and no shared state point to a subagent.

26
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:59,000
Think of a research stage that reads 20 files just to answer one question.

27
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:03,000
Nothing downstream needs those raw file contents,

28
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:07,000
so let a subagent burn that context and hand back only the answer.

29
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:15,000
Small steps whose details matter downstream stay in line, with state carried forward.

30
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:21,000
A step that renames a function, for example, produces changes every later step touches,

31
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:26,000
so isolating it would just force you to pass all that state back anyway.

32
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:31,000
Answer those two questions honestly, and the choice is usually clear.

33
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:36,000
The link that makes a chain clean is what each step hands to the next.

34
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:40,000
The rule is to forward the distilled result, not the whole working trace.

35
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:47,000
If a stage read 10 files to produce one decision, the next stage needs the decision,

36
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:48,000
not the 10 files.

37
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:52,000
So drop the scaffolding that later steps will never reference,

38
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:55,000
and give each stage only the inputs its own job requires.

39
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:01,000
Thin, deliberate handoffs are what keep the chain focused from start to finish.

40
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:07,000
When a workflow starts feeling sluggish or unfocused, look at your handoffs first.

41
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:11,000
Usually you're passing forward far more context than the next step actually uses.

42
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:17,000
Most multi-step reasoning today happens inside the model on its own,

43
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:20,000
so when should you chain steps explicitly?

44
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:22,000
Three reasons stand out.

45
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:26,000
First, when you want to inspect intermediate output,

46
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:30,000
seeing and checking a step's result before the next step runs on it.

47
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:35,000
Second, when you need to enforce a specific pipeline structure,

48
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:40,000
a repeatable sequence of stages, rather than letting the model improvise the path.

49
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:47,000
Third, when you want self-correction, running draft, then review, then refine as separate calls.

50
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:52,000
If none of these apply, you can often let the model handle the steps internally

51
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:53,000
and save yourself the plumbing.

52
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:57,000
Let's zoom in on that self-correction loop,

53
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:00,000
since it's the most common chaining pattern in practice.

54
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:02,000
It runs in three moves.

55
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:04,000
You draft a first answer to the task.

56
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:07,000
Then you review that draft against explicit criteria,

57
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:09,000
the same way you'd check a colleague's work.

58
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:15,000
Then you refine, fixing the specific issues the review surfaced.

59
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:21,000
The reason to run these as separate calls, instead of asking for it all at once, is control.

60
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:26,000
You can log each stage, evaluate it, and branch when something looks off.

61
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:31,000
That visibility is exactly what a single combined call hides from you,

62
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:33,000
and it's why chaining earns its keep here.

63
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:38,000
Three habits make your handoffs reliable, whatever the workflow.

64
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:41,000
The first is distill before passing.

65
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:46,000
Summarize each stage down to its load-bearing output before the next step ever sees it.

66
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:49,000
The second is validate between stages.

67
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:53,000
Check a stage's output against your rules before the following step builds on it,

68
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:56,000
so an error gets caught early instead of propagating.

69
00:04:57,000 --> 00:04:59,000
The third is keep steps independent.

70
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:05,000
Design each stage so it needs only its own inputs, not the entire history of the run.

71
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:09,000
Independent stages are easier to test, reorder, and debug.

72
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:15,000
Together, these three habits are what turn a fragile chain into one you can actually trust.

73
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:19,000
Let's close with where multi-step work goes wrong.

74
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:24,000
One failure is letting a single session grow instead of staging focused steps,

75
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:26,000
so the context slowly bloats.

76
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:32,000
Another is forwarding a full working trace instead of a distilled result,

77
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:35,000
which drags every step's noise into the next.

78
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:37,000
And a third is overchaining.

79
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:41,000
Breaking apart work the model's own internal reasoning would have handled fine,

80
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:45,000
which just adds plumbing and latency for no gain.

81
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:47,000
The through line is judgment.

82
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:51,000
Split when it buys you focus or control, not as a reflex.

83
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:55,000
The goal is focused context at every step,

84
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:58,000
and both too little structure and too much can cost you that.

85
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:05,000
Splitting a job into focused steps gives you a cleaner run than one growing session.

86
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:11,000
Each stage works against tight context and passes forward only its distilled output.

87
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:14,000
Chain explicitly when you need to inspect a step,

88
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:17,000
enforce a pipeline, or let the work self-correct.

89
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:21,000
That wraps up splitting work across multiple step workflows.

90
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:23,000
I'll see you in the next one.

